A method break does not make the banking series unusable; it changes the rule for comparing it. Where possible, compare observations within one statistical vintage and under the same reported treatment. Where a value for the same period is restated, keep both vintages visible until the bridge between them is understood.
Even within a consistent vintage, nominal balance-sheet growth does not by itself establish:
- real credit growth;
- more borrowers;
- wider access;
- better allocation of credit;
- stronger bank soundness;
- or greater financial inclusion.
Those are separate questions, each with its own evidence.

The exact January 2023 circular, together with a reconciliation table showing, for each affected balance-sheet line:
- the legacy value;
- the restated value;
- the valuation treatment;
- any change in classification or in the reporting population;
- and the part, if any, attributable to underlying economic movement.
Until that bridge exists, the safe rule is simple.
> Show the break. Keep the vintage. Do not calculate growth through a changed base as if the base had never moved.

A new representative measurement of people that keeps comparability on account ownership and adds:
- active and repeated digital use;
- saving and borrowing;
- the purposes of payments;
- barriers to opening an account;
- affordability and reliability;
- fraud and redress;
- receipt and use of remittances;
- gaps by gender and income;
- geography and displacement, where statistically supportable;
- persistence after programme participation or a transfer.
Read alongside sharper administrative data on unique users, frequency, reliability, cost and complaints, it would not force survey and administrative data into one number. It would build the bridge between what changed in the system and what changed for people.

Five disclosures would test the hypothesis against its alternatives:
- credit to the private sector by currency and by sector;
- loan applications and approvals, to separate weak demand from limited supply;
- a split of claims on government between frozen legacy treasury bills and other holdings;
- non-performing loans in a consistent series, on a stated perimeter, to test asset quality;
- a stated reporting perimeter: which banks, and which areas, the consolidated balance sheet covers.
> Until then, read the balance sheet as a picture of where assets sit, not as an account of why.

Before relying on these figures, a regulator, donor or provider can check four things:
- the unit: adults in surveyed areas, not households and not accounts;
- where the money moved: within Yemen in the survey, from abroad in CBY-Aden’s figures and the World Bank’s price data;
- the date: survey fieldwork ended in January 2023, and the only weighted channel values are for 2014; for the 2021 wave none usable here is published or held, though World Bank documentation records the item as asked and answered;
- the status of a provider: a roster entry shows a licence listed for 2026; it does not by itself show that the provider operates now.

Four kinds of evidence would test the hypothesis against its alternatives:
- a household survey module on remittances that records receipt, channel, currency and frequency;
- weighted channel estimates for domestic remittances: for the 2021 wave none usable here is published or held, though the World Bank documents that the question was asked and answered; and the World Bank has published no later Yemen wave;
- published figures, from exchange companies, remittance agents or their supervisor, that separate remittance pay-outs from currency exchange, by area;
- data that separate humanitarian cash from transfers between relatives in what households receive.
If adults who receive transfers received them into accounts or wallets of their own more often than through exchange companies and other money-transfer providers, where the two can be told apart, the hypothesis would fail.
> Until then, read the survey shares as the share of adults who sent or received money, not as how much households depend on it.
